<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.15.2: Using Tables and Graphs to Represent Geometric Sequences</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01133</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.15.2: Using Tables and Graphs to Represent Geometric Sequences</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fb84e946-8524-4c07-9167-f67b4fabfc33</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>Clare takes a piece of paper, cuts it in half, and then stacks the pieces. She takes the stack of two pieces and cuts
  it in half again to form four pieces, stacking them. She keeps repeating the process.</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>
    The original piece of paper has length 8 inches and width 10 inches. Complete the table.
 </item>
      </list>
      <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
        <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>number of cuts</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>number of pieces</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>area in square inches of each piece</emphasis>
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>
        0
      </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        1
      </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        2
      </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        3
      </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        4
      </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        5
      </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
  </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
              <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>number of cuts</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>number of pieces</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>area in square inches of each piece</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>
          0
        </entry>
                  <entry>1</entry>
                  <entry>80</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
          1
        </entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>40</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
          2
        </entry>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>20</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
          3
        </entry>
                  <entry>8</entry>
                  <entry>10</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
          4
        </entry>
                  <entry>16</entry>
                  <entry>5</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
          5
        </entry>
                  <entry>32</entry>
                  <entry>\(\frac52\) or 2.5</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>
        Describe in words how you can use the results after 5 cuts to find the results after 6 cuts.
    </para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Double the number of pieces, from 32 to 64. Halve the area of each piece, from \(\frac52\) to \(\frac54\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>
              <para>Use the graphing tool above or technology outside the course, on the given axes, sketch a graph of the
            number of pieces as a function of the number of cuts.

        </para>
          </para>
            <para>Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
  </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_18_03_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>How can you see on the graph how the number of pieces is changing with each cut?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The height of each plotted point is twice the height of the previous plotted point.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>
              <para>Use the graphing tool above or technology outside the course, on the given axes, sketch a graph of the
            area of each piece as a function of the number of cuts.
        </para>
          </para>
            <para>Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
  </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_18_03_02.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>How can you see how the area of each piece is changing with each cut?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The height of each plotted point is half the height of the previous plotted point.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            
            
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para>Clare has a piece of paper that is 8 inches by 10 inches.
        <para>How many pieces of paper will Clare have if she cuts the paper in half \(n\) times?</para>
      </para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(2^n\) pieces</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>
            <para>What will the area of each piece be?</para>
        </para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Each piece has an area of \(\frac{80}{2^n}\) square inches.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Why is the product of the number of pieces and the area of each piece always the same? Explain how you know.
    </para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>It’s always the same because adding up the area of the little pieces of paper gives the area of the
      original sheet of paper.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check"><title>Self Check</title>
      <para>Given the table below, what would be the next term in the sequence?</para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
        <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
            <entry><emphasis>
              \(x\)
           </emphasis> </entry><entry>
              1
            </entry>
            <entry>
              2
            </entry>
            <entry>
              3
            </entry>
            <entry>
              4
            </entry>
            <entry>
              5
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><emphasis>
              \(y\)
           </emphasis> </entry><entry>
              270
            </entry>
            <entry>
              90
            </entry>
            <entry>
              30
            </entry>
            <entry>
              10
            </entry>
            <entry>
              \(\frac{10}3\)
            </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table></note>
        <section>
          <title>Additional Resources</title>
          
    <section>
      <title>Graphs of Sequences</title>
      <para>Graph the sequence {18, 36, 72, 144, 288, . . .}.</para>
      <para>First, make a table:</para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
        <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>Term</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
        1
      </entry>
            <entry>
        2
      </entry>
            <entry>
        3
      </entry>
            <entry>
        4
      </entry>
            <entry>
        5
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>Value</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
        18
      </entry>
            <entry>
        36
      </entry>
            <entry>
        72
      </entry>
            <entry>
        144
      </entry>
            <entry>
        288
      </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <para>Now, plot the points:</para>
      <para>
          <media alt="Graph of the geometric sequence.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_18_05_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para>Notice that each height is double the previous height.</para>
      <para>Each term is also double the previous term.</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Graphs of Sequences</title>
      <para>Graph the following sequence and explain the pattern.</para>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>1, 3, 9, 27, . . .</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
  </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here is how to determine the pattern of a sequence with a graph:</para>
            <para>First, make a table:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
              <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>Term</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
          1
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          2
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          3
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          4
        </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>Value</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
          1
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          3
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          9
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          127
        </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para>Next, plot the points:</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="GRAPH SHOWING PLOTTED POINTS (1, 1), (2, 3), (3, 9), AND (4, 27).">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_18_05_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para>Each point is 3 times the height of the previous point.</para>
            <para>Notice that in the sequence, each term is 3 times the previous term.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
        </section>
        
